Executive Summary

Building a fully autonomous Polymarket trading bot for small capital (≈$20–$200) requires integrating Polymarket’s unique hybrid architecture with robust strategy and risk controls. Polymarket runs a hybrid CLOB (Central Limit Order Book) on Polygon: orders are matched off-chain by the operator, while settlements occur on-chain via smart contracts. This non-custodial design means the bot signs transactions locally (EIP-712) and retains control of funds, but must handle a two-tier authentication (L1 wallet signature and L2 API key) for trading. No trading fees apply on most markets, but short-duration crypto markets charge taker fees (up to ~1.5%) to fund a maker rebate program – making liquidity-providing strategies more attractive for a small bot. Market outcomes are resolved via UMA’s Optimistic Oracle, introducing a delay (usually 2+ hours) for disputes, so the bot’s capital may be briefly locked at resolution.

The bot will operate 24/7, ingesting real-time market data through Polymarket’s WebSocket feeds (≈100ms latency updates) and REST APIs for initialization. A minimal MVP (7-day) implementation will focus on connectivity, data handling, and a paper-trading strategy to simulate market making. A subsequent V1 (30-day) rollout will enable live trading with micro-capital, modular strategies, and thorough testing/monitoring. Strategies center on market making (quoting both sides to earn the bid-ask spread and maker rebates), internal arbitrage (exploiting mispricings like YES+NO < $1.00 when available), and event-driven trades (reacting to news) – all adapted to the constraints of small bankroll and higher relative transaction costs. Risk management is paramount: the plan includes strict exposure limits, circuit-breakers to halt on abnormal losses or errors, and recovery procedures to handle outages or delayed resolutions. Compliance is also integral – the bot will only operate in permitted regions (Polymarket geoblocks 33 countries including the US, UK, etc.) and monitor for any cert_required flags requiring user verification. Development will use a “vibe coding” methodology, leveraging AI pair-programming (ChatGPT/Copilot) to implement each module iteratively with prompt-driven guidance.

Overall, this report provides a deep technical blueprint – from architecture and strategy design to a step-by-step build plan, risk safeguards, and deployment considerations – for launching a small-cap Polymarket trading bot with confidence and compliance.

Architecture Overview

Hybrid CLOB Structure: Polymarket’s exchange is a hybrid of off-chain speed and on-chain security. An off-chain matching engine maintains the order book and matching logic, while actual trade settlements occur on Polygon (chain id 137) via smart contracts. Users deposit USDC into Polymarket’s contract (Gnosis Conditional Token Framework) and receive outcome tokens (Yes/No shares). The bot will not custody funds on a centralized server; instead it will sign orders locally using the wallet’s private key (EIP-712 signed messages) and send them to the CLOB API. This ensures the operator cannot move funds without valid signatures (all trades remain non-custodial). If needed, orders can even be canceled on-chain by the user as a trust fallback. The order types supported are all variants of limit orders (market orders are achieved by crossing the spread with a limit price) – including Good-til-Cancelled (GTC), Good-til-Date (auto-expire by a timestamp), Fill-or-Kill (must fill entirely or cancel), and Fill-and-Kill (Immediate-Or-Cancel partial fill). For example, a FOK order will attempt to buy/sell a full amount immediately or not at all – useful for arbitrage to ensure both legs fill. Post-only orders are also available to ensure the bot’s orders only add liquidity and never take liquidity (if a post-only order would cross and fill, it’s rejected).

L1/L2 Authentication: The bot must navigate Polymarket’s two-level auth system. L1 auth uses the Ethereum private key to sign a challenge (an EIP-712 message with wallet address, timestamp, nonce) for the API – proving control of the wallet. This is required once to create or derive API credentials. Using an official SDK method like createOrDeriveApiKey() generates a set of L2 API credentials (an API key, secret, and passphrase) tied to the wallet. Thereafter, the bot uses L2 auth (HMAC-SHA256 signatures) on each trading request. In practice, the bot will instantiate a client with the wallet signer and call createOrDeriveApiKey to get creds, or load existing creds from config if already generated. Public endpoints (market data, order books, prices) require no auth tokens – the bot can freely fetch market info. But private endpoints (placing or canceling orders, viewing account fills) require L2 headers: POLY_API_KEY, a timestamp, passphrase, and a signature (HMAC of request) using the secret. The separation means the wallet key is only used sparingly (reducing risk) while regular trading uses API keys. All credentials will be stored securely (never committed to code, only in environment variables or a vault, as also advised in official docs).

API and WebSocket Handling: Polymarket provides multiple APIs which the bot will leverage for different purposes: the Gamma API (REST) for market metadata and discovery (e.g. list markets, categories, status), the CLOB REST API for on-demand order book snapshots and order placement, and the CLOB WebSocket for real-time streaming of market data and personal order updates. The WebSocket connection (wss://ws-subscriptions-clob.polymarket.com) is critical for low-latency updates – it streams order book changes (book and price_change events), tick size changes, last trades, etc., in near real-time. The bot will subscribe to the market channel for each relevant market’s order book, and the user channel (with L2 auth) for its own order and trade confirmations. Handling the WebSocket involves an event loop: e.g., on receiving a book snapshot or incremental update, update the local order book data structure; on order_filled events, adjust positions. A robust design will include auto-reconnect logic – if the socket drops or no ping/pong is received, the bot should pause trading, reconnect with exponential backoff, and resynchronize (e.g. fetch a fresh order book snapshot via REST once reconnected to avoid missing any updates). The REST endpoints provide fallbacks and additional data: for example, GET /markets/{id} for market details, GET /book?token_id=X for full order book snapshot, and POST /order to place orders. The bot should minimize REST calls during live trading (to avoid hitting rate limits), relying on the WebSocket for continuous data. Notably, Polymarket’s API has rate limits (undocumented officially, but community sources suggest ~100 public requests/min and ~60 orders/min per API key). The bot will incorporate request throttling and batching (the API allows posting up to 15 orders in one batch) to stay within limits and handle any 429 Too Many Requests responses via backoff retries.

Tick Size Enforcement: Prices on Polymarket are discrete, constrained by a tick size (minimum increment) that can change based on price range. Typically the tick is $0.01 for most mid-range prices, but it tightens to $0.001 when the price goes above 0.96 or below 0.04 (markets near certainty or impossibility get finer granularity), and some markets even use $0.0001 at extremes. The bot must always query the current tick size (GET /tick-size?token_id=X) before placing an order, to ensure its price is an exact multiple of that tick size – otherwise the order will be rejected as invalid. Polymarket’s API will throw an "INVALID_ORDER_MIN_TICK_SIZE" error if the price doesn’t conform to the tick grid. A known pitfall is caching a tick size: if the market moves and triggers a tick change (e.g. from 0.01 to 0.001), a bot using an outdated tick value would place incorrect prices. To avoid this, our bot will update tick sizes on-the-fly (the WebSocket even emits a tick_size_change event when this happens) and validate order prices against the current tick. Minimum order size per market is another constraint (often 0.001 or 0.01 shares); the bot should refrain from orders smaller than the allowed size (the API returns "INVALID_ORDER_MIN_SIZE" if violated). With small capital, it’s unlikely we go below min size, but the bot will double-check the min_order_size from market data to be safe.

Fee Structure and Rebates: Polymarket historically did not charge trading fees, but as of late 2025 introduced fees on certain markets. The vast majority of markets remain fee-free – no fees to trade, deposit, or withdraw. However, 15-minute interval crypto markets (very short-term markets that settle frequently, e.g. “Will BTC go up in the next 15 minutes”) now have taker fees on each trade, up to ~1.5% at 50-50 odds. These taker fees are not taken by Polymarket as profit but are pooled into a Maker Rebates Program, meaning market makers earn rebates in USDC proportional to the volume and quality of liquidity they provide. For the bot, this implies: if it sticks to normal prediction markets (sports, politics, daily markets, etc.), it pays 0 fees on trades (so it can even cross the spread without a direct fee, though crossing still incurs the spread cost). On the other hand, if it trades in the fee-enabled crypto micro-markets, it should avoid taking liquidity – paying 1%+ fee as a taker on a small $20 trade is significant. Instead, the strategy should provide liquidity (post resting limit orders) to earn the rebate. Rebates are paid daily in USDC, typically requiring a minimum volume to notice meaningful amounts. The bot can query the fee rate via GET /fee-rate?token_id=X if needed to know if a market has fees and include the feeRateBps in signed orders. Initially, a small-cap bot will likely focus on fee-free markets or maker-only on fee markets to avoid the taker cost. (Polymarket’s documentation emphasizes that 15-min markets have a “small taker fee on each trade… redistributed daily to market makers as rebates”.) This fee dynamic strongly shapes our strategy selection (as discussed in the Strategy section).

UMA Oracle and Resolution Delays: Polymarket uses the UMA Optimistic Oracle (OO) to resolve outcome markets trustlessly. When a market’s event outcome is determined (e.g. after an election or a sports match ends), any user can propose a resolution (Yes or No) on-chain by posting a proposal bond (usually $750 USDC as collateral). The outcome doesn’t resolve immediately; instead there’s a challenge period (typically 2 hours) for anyone to dispute that outcome if they disagree. If no one disputes, the proposed outcome is accepted and the market is settled shortly after liveness ends. If a dispute is raised (by someone else also putting up an equal bond), the question escalates to UMA’s DVM (decentralized verification mechanism) for a formal vote by UMA token holders – which can take an additional 48–96 hours to finalize. In normal cases with no disputes, resolution happens the same day (just a few hours delay). In worst-case contentious outcomes, the bot’s funds could be tied up for a few days until final resolution. Bot implications: the bot should monitor resolution status for markets it’s active in – via the Polymarket Gamma API (markets have fields indicating open/closed/resolved) or even by listening to on-chain events. Once a market resolves, any winning shares turn into $1 USDC each, and losing shares to $0. The bot should automatically redeem its winning shares to realize the profits (this might be done via Polymarket UI or an on-chain call if not automatic – official docs hint that manual redemption may be needed by the user to claim the USDC, which the bot can automate if an API exists for it). Additionally, the strategy might avoid tying up too much capital in markets that are near expiration, or at least be aware that right after an event, its capital might not be immediately available for new trades until UMA’s window passes. Planning for a “settlement buffer” is wise – e.g. not allocating 100% of funds into a single market that might go into a 2-day dispute, to keep some capital liquid for other opportunities. Overall, the UMA resolution mechanism adds decentralization and safety, but the bot must handle the asynchronous nature of settlement (no instant payout upon event conclusion, unlike a centralized exchange).

Strategy Models (with Edge Cases)

Designing a profitable strategy for a small-cap Polymarket bot involves combining market-proven tactics with adaptations for low capital and Polymarket’s specific microstructure. We outline several strategy models and key edge-case considerations:

Market Making & Liquidity Provision

Description: The bot acts as a market maker, continuously placing buy and sell limit orders (quotes) around the current fair price (midpoint). The goal is to earn the bid-ask spread repeatedly and collect any maker rebates or liquidity rewards Polymarket offers. This is well-suited to Polymarket’s fee model (no taker fees on most markets, and rebates on some) and to small capital (it avoids aggressive position-taking). Essentially, the bot will maintain two-sided orders: for example, if the fair probability of an event is 0.50 (50%), the bot might place a buy at 0.49 and a sell at 0.51, profiting ~0.02 spread if both fill (each share bought at $0.49 can later be sold at $0.51). Even if only one side fills, the bot then re-centers and continues making markets.

Small-Capital Adaptations: With only $20–$200, the bot must deploy capital carefully. It should focus on 1–3 markets maximum to avoid overextending. It will also use smaller order sizes (maybe $5–$20 per order) to stay flexible and avoid being too much of the market (which could attract bigger traders to pick off its quotes). The spreads it sets will likely be wider than large-market makers – e.g. aiming for a 1–3% spread instead of 0.1–0.5% – because the bot cannot afford to race for razor-thin margins given Polygon gas costs and potential price jumps. Additionally, to minimize on-chain cancel costs, the bot can use passive order management: for instance, use GTC orders that remain until filled, and only cancel/replace them when the price moves significantly beyond a threshold. Community bot developers noted that over-canceling orders can waste up to 95% of gas costs for no gain. Our bot will implement cooldowns on cancel/replace frequency and prefer adjusting prices gradually, especially when network gas is high.

Edge Cases & Risks: Market making is not risk-free. Inventory risk is a primary concern: if one side of the quote keeps getting hit, the bot accumulates a position. For example, if the bot keeps buying “YES” shares at falling prices (because the event is becoming less likely), it could end up with a large long position in a losing outcome. We will impose inventory limits (e.g. max X shares or Y% of capital on one side). If the limit is hit, the bot can widen spreads or pause quoting on that side to avoid further accumulation. Volatility spikes are another edge case: if sudden news shifts the fair value, the bot’s quotes could become far off and get “picked off” by others for an instant loss (e.g. bot had a sell at 0.51 and news makes true price 0.60 – the bot sells too cheap). To mitigate this, the bot will use dynamic spreads (widen spreads when short-term volatility is detected) and possibly a “band” strategy: only quote within a certain band of recent prices and cancel if the market moves outside that band until a new equilibrium is found. An extreme scenario: a bot without stop-loss could lose a large chunk if a market moves strongly one way – community anecdote mentions a bot incurring ~35% loss in seconds due to a price spike on low liquidity (no protective stops). Our bot will address this with stop-loss logic or circuit breakers (discussed in Risk Management). Also, while Polymarket has liquidity incentives (rebates, rewards for makers), these are relatively small – maybe on the order of a few percent APY for heavy providers. The bot shouldn’t chase rebate rewards at the expense of good risk/reward decisions; the rebates are a bonus, but actual spread capture and eventual price move profits are the main income.

Internal Arbitrage (YES+NO < 1 and Related Opportunities)

Description: In a fairly priced binary market, the best YES price plus NO price should roughly equal $1.00 (since one of them will pay $1 at resolution). If the sum is less than $1, there’s a potential arbitrage: the bot can buy both outcomes and lock in a risk-free profit (paying e.g. $0.98 total for one YES and one NO share, knowing one will be worth $1, yielding $0.02 profit). Similarly, if the sum is over $1 (e.g. yes at 0.60, no at 0.45 = $1.05), a bot could short both sides (sell them) to capture $1.05 now and only owe $1 on resolution (net $0.05 profit). However, shorting on Polymarket is not straightforward (it would require borrowing or using the conditional tokens framework to create shares), so our focus is on the YES+NO < 1 case (also called a “Dutch book” arbitrage). The bot would monitor order books for instances where due to imbalances or stale orders, the best bid on one side plus best ask on the other side < 1.00 (minus fees). For example, if someone placed a very cheap YES ask at 0.40 while NO is 0.58 bid, the sum is 0.98 – the bot can lift the YES ask at 0.40 and hit the NO ask (i.e., buy NO at 0.42 if available) or just buy YES and NO at those prices, investing $0.98 for a guaranteed $1 payoff. This is a self-contained arbitrage on Polymarket.

Edge Cases & Practicality: In practice, pure arbitrage opportunities are rare and small on Polymarket. The presence of the spread and (if applicable) fees usually keeps YES+NO ≥ $1. The bot might find occasional moments right after a sudden move or in a very illiquid market. It must be fast to capitalize (other traders or bots will too). A critical risk is execution lag: the bot needs both legs (Yes and No) to fill. Using FOK order type is wise – e.g., submit a FOK buy for YES and a FOK buy for NO simultaneously, so they either both fill completely or not at all. If only one side fills and the other doesn’t (leg risk), the bot ends up with an unhedged position. For instance, it buys a lot of YES at 0.40 expecting to also buy NO at 0.60, but NO order fails and then price of YES collapses – this turns a supposed arbitrage into a directional loss. Our strategy will include logic to immediately cancel the filled leg or exit if the complementary leg fails, possibly taking a very small loss to unwind, rather than staying unhedged. Additionally, we must consider fees: if one leg is taker and the market has a fee, it could wipe out the profit. Thus the bot will prioritize arbitrage in 0% fee markets.

Another internal arbitrage pattern is cross-market consistency: Polymarket might have related markets (e.g. “Candidate A wins election” and “Candidate B wins election” – only one can be true, or markets that are subsets of each other). If the prices diverge from logical relationships, the bot could trade to correct them. However, these opportunities are complex and often constrained by liquidity and time. With limited capital, the bot will not engage heavily in multi-market arbitrage beyond the straightforward sum-of-shares check within the same market or obvious mispricing between very closely related markets.

Event-Driven Trading (News Reaction Patterns)

Description: This strategy attempts to capitalize on breaking news or events that affect a market’s outcome probability. For example, if a major poll or report comes out that should sway a prediction market (say, a sudden development in a legal case for a Polymarket on that outcome), the bot tries to trade on that information before the market fully adjusts. Typically, this involves feeding the bot external data – e.g. monitoring Twitter, news APIs, or using Polymarket’s Real-Time Data Stream (RTDS) which may include news sentiment or user comments. On detecting a significant news signal (like “Candidate X drops out of race”), the bot would aggressively buy or sell the outcome tokens before the price moves to a new equilibrium.

Small-Capital Adaptation: Pure low-latency arbitrage is tough with small capital and cloud hosting: larger players might have colocated servers or faster feeds. Our bot cannot realistically beat professional firms to very time-sensitive news in highly competitive markets. Instead, it can focus on slower event trades – situations where information diffusion isn’t instant or where human traders overreact/underreact and a bot can systematically trade the reversion. For instance, the bot could identify over-corrections (if price jumps from 0.50 to 0.80 on a rumor and then falls back to 0.60, a contrarian bot could short at 0.80 with stop if it keeps rising). The bot should avoid chasing every spike. Latency considerations: Polygon block times (~2 seconds) and webSocket ~100-200ms mean that by the time our bot sees a big trade, it’s already happened. Thus, a reactive bot often ends up behind the curve, buying after the price has moved. To mitigate this, the bot could place some contingent orders ahead of time – e.g., a cheap buy order far below market in case of a flash crash, or a sell order above in case of a spike, essentially acting as a reserve liquidity provider during volatility.

Edge Cases: The biggest risk is false signals – acting on noise. For example, an unreliable tweet might cause a quick pump; if the bot buys in and it turns out to be fake, the price could crash and the bot loses. Tight stop-loss rules (or quickly reversing the trade) are needed. Also, slippage is a problem: if the bot tries to market buy after news, the order book might be thin and it ends up paying a much higher price than intended (or selling much lower). Using limit orders with FOK/IOC can limit slippage by only filling at acceptable prices or not at all. Another edge scenario: no liquidity – if a market has very little depth and the bot trades on news, it might move the price by itself significantly (thus negating profit). The strategy should include a filter like “only trade event if market has > $X liquidity on book”. Taker fees (if in a fee market) also heavily weigh on this strategy – an event-driven trade often requires taking liquidity now, so in a 15-minute market the bot would pay a fee. If the edge is small, the fee can erase it. Therefore, if doing event trades, the bot will prefer markets with 0% fee or factor the fee into the decision (only act if the probability change is well above the fee threshold).

In summary, event-driven patterns are possible but highly cautious for this bot. It will likely be used sparingly, possibly during known scheduled events (debates, announcements) where the bot can prepare, rather than pure surprise news which require ultra-fast reaction that our small setup may not consistently achieve.

Overall Risk Management

Across all strategies, universal risk controls will be in place to handle edge cases and prevent catastrophic losses. Key measures include:

Position Limits: The bot will enforce per-market and overall exposure limits. For example, it might limit to at most 10% of total capital on any single outcome (ensuring diversification and that one bad market doesn’t wipe out everything). It will also cap the absolute number of shares held per market (inventory control) as mentioned for market making.

Stop-Loss and Take-Profit: For directional trades (event-driven or if the bot decides to hold a position), predefined stop-loss triggers will close the position if it moves against the bot beyond a threshold (e.g. cut losses at -20%). Similarly, it could take profit once a trade achieves a certain gain instead of getting greedy. Market making positions are a bit different (they are meant to be offset by the opposite order filling), but if the market moves strongly one way, a stop-loss can effectively act as a circuit breaker to cancel all quotes and possibly exit any remaining positions.

Circuit Breakers: Beyond individual stops, the bot will have global circuit breakers – e.g., if daily net PnL goes below a certain dollar loss, the bot will halt trading (it indicates something’s wrong or market conditions changed drastically). Other breakers could include detecting if the market becomes too volatile (e.g. >30% price swing in a minute) and pausing that market, or if the WebSocket data is lagging (no updates for a while or too many reconnects) and thus the bot might be trading blind – then pause until it’s resolved.

Testing and Dry Runs: Before live deployment, the strategies will be tested extensively (discussed later). The bot will run in paper trading mode for days to ensure its logic actually yields positive results and behaves correctly on edge cases (e.g., resolution events, fast markets). Only after proving safe in simulation will real money be put at stake, starting at the lowest end of capital (maybe $20 or even $10) and scaling up gradually as confidence grows.

By combining careful strategy selection with these layered safeguards, the bot aims to steadily grow a small bankroll without succumbing to the many pitfalls that can occur in autonomous trading (like API glitches, wild price events, or simply being outgunned by bigger fish). The next sections will outline how to implement this step-by-step, and how to keep the system reliable and compliant over time.

Full MVP + V1 Build Plan

We adopt a two-stage development approach using vibe coding (AI-assisted) iteration: first a 7-day MVP focusing on core functionality and simulation, then a 30-day V1 to go live with real trades and robust features. Each step is structured with clear goals (and can be accompanied by a GPT/Copilot prompt to implement it). The plan assumes using TypeScript (Node.js) for the bot, given the well-supported Polymarket SDK in TS and strong typing – though Python is also possible, we choose TS for this roadmap due to fewer known issues. Key modules (data handling, strategy logic, risk management, etc.) will be developed incrementally. Below is the timeline with major milestones:

MVP (Week 1) – 7-Day Development Sprint

Day 1: Project Setup & Basic Data Fetch – Initialize a Git repository and Node project. Install essential libraries: Polymarket’s CLOB SDK (@polymarket/clob-client), ethers (for signing), and utilities like dotenv (for config), a logger (e.g. pino). Set up a config file to hold API URL, chain ID, and placeholders for keys. Implement a simple CLOB API client wrapper that can fetch a market’s order book via REST. Test: Run a small script to fetch the order book of a known active market and log the top of book (best bid/ask) to confirm connectivity.

Day 2: Real-time WebSocket Integration – Implement the Market WebSocket connector. This entails creating a module that opens a WebSocket to Polymarket’s market channel and subscribes to one or more market IDs for live updates. Handle incoming messages: on a full book snapshot, store it; on incremental updates (price_change events), update the in-memory order book data structure accordingly. The order book can be maintained as sorted lists or heaps of bids and asks. Also include basic heartbeat (send periodic pings as required, e.g. every 10 seconds, to keep connection alive). Test: Subscribe to a volatile market for ~5 minutes and log changes (e.g. print mid-price each time it updates) to ensure the flow of updates is correct.

Day 3: Paper Trading Strategy Module – Develop a Paper Market Maker strategy class. This will use the live order book from Day 2 but instead of placing real orders, it simulates orders. Implement logic such that on each tick (or on each book update): decide a target bid and ask price (e.g. around the mid or last trade price with a fixed spread % or offset), and “simulate” placing orders. Keep track of these simulated orders and fill them if the market moves through their price. For example, if our simulated ask is at $0.55 and a buy trade happens at $0.55 or above, assume our ask filled. Update a virtual position and record the trade. Essentially, this module behaves like a market maker, but uses the live market data to generate fills. Also track PnL: realized gains from spreads earned, and unrealized (the mark-to-market value of any open position, though a pure market maker might try to be mostly flat). Test: Run the paper trader on a market for 1 hour. Log periodic PnL and ensure it makes sense (e.g., if the market oscillated and both buy and sell simulated orders executed, PnL should reflect the spreads earned).

Day 4: Persistence & Logging – Set up a lightweight database (SQLite for simplicity during MVP) to store trade logs, PnL history, etc. Create tables for simulated trades and for PnL snapshots over time. Integrate the database such that whenever the paper strategy “executes” a fill, we insert a trade record (timestamp, market, side, price, size). Also, take a snapshot of PnL every N minutes into a table for later analysis. In parallel, add structured logging using a logger library, ensuring all key events (connection issues, orders, fills, risk triggers) are logged with timestamps. Test: After running the strategy, verify that the SQLite DB has recorded the expected number of trades and that querying it shows plausible values. Check that log files contain the expected info.

Day 5: Risk Management Features – Even in paper mode, implement core risk checks. Create a RiskLimits module that can, given a potential new order or position, validate it against preset limits (like max position per market and max total exposure). Integrate this so the paper trader will not “place” orders that would violate limits (e.g., if it already has 100 shares, and max is 100, it won’t place further orders on that side). Next, implement a basic CircuitBreaker mechanism: e.g., if realized PnL drops below -X in a session, or if any other serious condition, it sets a flag to halt trading. In paper mode, we can simulate triggering it by injecting a fake loss. Test: Manually adjust a variable or simulate a scenario to trip the circuit breaker (like call a function to decrement PnL by a large amount), and verify that the strategy stops placing new orders for a set cooldown period. Ensure that risk limit checks prevent over-exposure (e.g., try a scenario where price trends one way and see that the bot doesn’t keep adding beyond the cap).

Day 6: Monitoring & Alerts – Integrate metrics and alerting. Add a Prometheus metrics endpoint (HTTP server on a local port) exposing key metrics such as current PnL, open positions, number of orders placed, etc. Use a library like prom-client to define gauges/counters for these. Simultaneously, set up a simple alerting module: e.g., use a Telegram bot API or Discord webhook to send messages when critical events occur (circuit breaker tripped, or an unexpected error). On the ops side, prepare a Grafana dashboard (if using Grafana Cloud, configure the Prometheus data source, or use a local Grafana via docker). Test: Confirm that hitting the /metrics endpoint returns the metrics in Prometheus format, and that an alert message is sent (perhaps simulate a circuit break or send a test alert).

Day 7: Documentation and Final Checks – Write a clear README and inline documentation. The README will describe how to configure and run the bot, the architecture (with perhaps an ASCII diagram of the system flow), and instructions for moving to live trading. Include an .env.example file for required environment variables (private key, API key placeholders, etc.). Ensure the code is well-commented (using JSDoc or similar for important functions). Finally, run a 24-hour stress test of the MVP in paper mode: let it run on a few active markets to see if it remains stable (no memory leaks, no crashes). This will shake out any issues with reconnection, etc., in a long-running scenario. The success criteria for MVP include: staying connected and syncing for a full day, collecting PnL data, and handling any minor disconnects gracefully.

By the end of Day 7, we have a bot that connects to real market data and simulates trading with risk controls and monitoring, all documented – essentially a dry-run version of the eventual live bot.

V1 (Weeks 2–4) – 30-Day Roadmap to Live Trading

Week 2 (Days 8–14): Enable Real Trading

Days 8–10: Wallet Integration and Order Signing. Upgrade the bot to interact with real orders. Implement a module for EIP-712 order signing. This involves constructing the order payload (with fields like maker address, tokenId, amount, expiration, fee rate, etc.) and signing it using the wallet’s private key (through ethers or the Polymarket SDK). Using the SDK’s ClobClient can simplify some of this (it provides methods to create orders given parameters). Ensure the bot can generate the required signature and include it in the POST /order call. Also, perform the Polymarket L1 auth step: using createOrDeriveApiKey() with our signer to obtain API credentials (if not already done manually). Securely store these L2 creds for reuse. Test: Try a dry run where the bot attempts to create an order (perhaps hitting a sandbox or a test mode). If there’s no testnet, we can set a very low-size order on a real market that we immediately cancel. The goal is to verify the signing process is correct and the API responds with success for order placement (or at least we get a plausible response).

Days 11–14: Order Placement & Cancellation Logic. Build an OrderManager component responsible for sending orders to Polymarket and handling responses. Incorporate features like: batching multiple orders into one request if needed (Polymarket allows up to 15 in one call), handling partial fills or rejections, and triggering cancels. Also implement cancellation functions – e.g., cancel all open orders on a market or cancel a specific order by ID. Integrate tick size checks here: before posting, validate the price against current tick size (to avoid the immediate rejections discussed earlier). Rate-limit the order submissions to avoid hitting the 60 orders/min limit (if the strategy is very active, queue and space out order sends). Test: Place a small live order in a quiet market (perhaps price it so it doesn’t actually trade, e.g., a bid far below market), then use the cancel function to remove it. Ensure the order goes live on the book (check via API or UI) and then is successfully canceled. This proves the bot can interact with the real exchange.

Week 3 (Days 15–21): Fills, Positions, and Resilience

Days 15–18: User WebSocket & Fill Processing. Activate the user channel WebSocket to get real-time feed of the bot’s own orders and trades. Upon subscribing with our API key and secret, the bot will receive messages whenever an order is created, filled, partially filled, or canceled (by the system or manually) on our account. Implement handlers for these events: e.g., when an order event says an order is now live or canceled, update our local order state; when a trade event indicates a fill, update the position and PnL. The bot should maintain an internal Position Tracker mapping each market to how many shares of Yes/No it holds and average cost, etc. This ensures at any time we know our exposure. Test: Submit a test order and let it execute (maybe cross the spread with a tiny order so it fills immediately). Verify that the user WebSocket trade message is received and the position is updated accordingly in the bot’s state.

Days 19–21: Crash Recovery & State Sync. Build a robust startup routine so that if the bot restarts (or crashes and is restarted), it can reconstruct the world state before resuming trading. This involves: on launch, calling the API to get all open orders for the account and all current positions (which can be derived from holdings of conditional tokens – possibly via the subgraph or summing past trades). Reconcile this with our database or last saved state: if the bot thought an order was open but it’s not in the open list, mark it as filled or canceled appropriately. Similarly, load any persisted PnL or trade history needed. Essentially, the bot should be able to recover from a cold start without confusion about what it holds or what orders are pending. Implement saving state to disk if needed (though the DB and the exchange are sources of truth). Test: While running the bot live (in a controlled way), restart the process. After restart, ensure it prints out the correct currently open orders (matching what was there pre-restart) and correct positions. This test proves that if the bot or server restarts (or even if we deploy an update), it can pick up where it left off and not, say, double-count positions or forget to cancel stale orders.

Week 4 (Days 22–30): Live Trading & Hardening

Days 22–25: Deploy the Market Making Strategy Live. Migrate the earlier paper trading strategy to an actual Live Market Maker module. This involves using the OrderManager instead of simulated orders: when strategy decides to quote prices, it will send real orders through. The logic will be similar (computing bid/ask around mid, adjusting for inventory). Implement spread adjustments and order replacement: e.g., if the market price moves, the bot should cancel outdated quotes and place new ones at the new desired levels. Care must be taken to avoid too-frequent cancel/replace (to save gas). Possibly implement a tolerance band (don’t immediately cancel unless price moved more than X%). Also ensure the strategy respects the risk limits (it should call risk validation before sending any order). Start with very small size (perhaps $5 or $10 worth of shares total) to test the waters. Test/Observation: Run the live strategy on one market for 24 hours with minimal capital. We expect to see a few fills (depending on market activity) – verify the PnL computation matches what actual trades occurred (including any fees or rebate, which might be seen only after a day). This will likely require manual verification or using the Polymarket UI to see if positions grew/shrank correctly.

Days 26–28: Multi-Market Support. Extend the bot to handle multiple markets concurrently. Generalize the strategy to either run separate instances per market or a unified loop that iterates through markets. Add a config file listing which markets (by token ID) to trade, plus their strategy parameters (e.g. some markets might have different spread targets or position limits). Introduce a simple capital allocation – e.g., split capital equally among N markets, or have a per-market max allocation. Ensure the risk management now checks aggregate exposure across markets as well (so the total used capital doesn’t exceed our budget). Test: Configure 2 or 3 markets (perhaps one crypto, one sports, one politics for diversity) with tiny allocations and run the bot for 2 days. Observe that it maintains separate order books and positions correctly, and that it doesn’t exceed the overall capital (for example, if one market uses less, that free capital isn’t mistakenly double-used in another due to accounting errors).

Days 29–30: System Hardening and Review. In the final days, focus on bug fixes, optimizations, and chaos testing. Induce some failure scenarios: e.g., deliberately drop the WebSocket connection (maybe disconnect internet or kill the socket) and see if reconnect logic recovers the feed and the bot resumes trading properly. Test how the bot behaves if the Polymarket API returns an error or if an order is rejected – does it retry gracefully and log the issue? Monitor resource usage over extended runs (memory, CPU) to catch any leaks or inefficiencies. Implement any performance improvements as needed (for instance, if processing every tick update is too slow, maybe throttle how often the strategy recomputes quotes). Finally, verify the monitoring and alerting end-to-end: trigger a fake alert and see it appear on Telegram/Discord, ensure metrics are flowing to Grafana, etc. By Day 30, compile all documentation (update the README with any new setup steps for live trading, like how to input API keys and manage secrets). At this point, V1 success criteria should be met: the bot can trade live with minimal supervision, maintain uptime, handle multiple markets, and provide introspection via metrics/logs.

This completes the V1 build. The result is a fully functional, if somewhat minimalist, Polymarket trading bot. It can be further improved beyond 30 days (adding more sophisticated strategies, scaling to more capital, etc.), but even at V1, it embodies an end-to-end system: from data ingestion and decision-making to real trade execution, risk mitigation, and monitoring. Throughout development, we have leveraged “vibe coding” with AI suggestions for each module, accelerating the coding of boilerplate and letting us focus on fine-tuning strategy logic.

Final Runbook (SRE + Incident Handling)

Even after development, running a bot 24/7 requires Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) practices and an incident response plan. This runbook outlines how to operate and maintain the bot in production, including start/stop procedures, health checks, failure recovery, and emergency actions.

Startup Sequence

Environment Prep: Ensure all environment variables are set (private key, API key/secret/passphrase, etc.) and configuration files (markets list, risk limits) are in place. Verify the machine’s clock is in sync (for accurate timestamps).

Dependency Check: Start the database (if using an external DB) or verify file-based DB is accessible. Ensure internet connectivity to Polygon network and Polymarket APIs.

Bot Launch: Run the bot process (e.g. via npm start or a systemd service). On launch, the bot will log initial settings.

Funds & Allowances Check: The bot should first check the wallet’s USDC balance on Polygon – if it’s below a safe minimum (including gas for transactions), it will log an error and stop to prevent running without funds. It also should confirm that the ERC-20 allowance for USDC to Polymarket’s contract is set sufficiently (and set it via on-chain txn if not). Similarly, ensure any required ERC-1155 approvals (for conditional tokens) are in place.

Authentication: The bot performs L1 authentication if needed to generate L2 creds (or loads existing creds). It then tests an authenticated call (like GET /orders or a small POST /order with postOnly and immediate cancel) to ensure creds are valid.

State Reconstruction: The bot fetches open orders from the API and current positions (by querying the user’s token balances or trade history) to sync state. It reconciles any discrepancies with its local database, updating the local order and position records.

Market Data Connections: Connect to the market WebSocket channel for all markets of interest, and the user WebSocket channel for personal updates. Upon connecting, subscribe to the required market IDs. The bot may also do an initial REST fetch of each order book to have a baseline before streaming updates.

Strategy Activation: Once data is flowing and state is synced, the bot enters its main strategy loop. It logs a “READY” or similar message indicating it’s now actively trading.

Metrics Endpoint: The Prometheus metrics server should be started and listening (by this point, initial metrics like “bot_start_time” can be set). External monitoring systems can poll this.

Continuous Logging: All through startup, any issues or warnings (e.g. low balance, high latency on connections) are logged. If any critical step fails (like unable to auth or open WS), the bot will attempt retries a few times; if still failing, it will log a fatal error and shut down cleanly (or disable trading functionality) awaiting human intervention.

Main Loop Operation

The bot’s main loop has both event-driven and interval-driven components:

Real-time events: Most actions occur on incoming WebSocket messages. For instance, on a book_update or price_change event, the strategy’s algorithm will recalculate desired orders (quote adjustments) and possibly send new orders or cancels. On an order_filled event (from the user feed), the position tracker updates the holdings and realized PnL, and the strategy might adjust its quotes or inventory targets accordingly.

Periodic ticks: Additionally, a timed loop (say every 10 seconds) triggers the strategy to evaluate if it needs to quote or requote even in absence of outside events. This ensures, for example, if nothing has happened for a while, the bot still refreshes orders or computes if it should change something (perhaps to slowly move toward a target inventory or to enforce a time-based cancel/replace). In this tick, the strategy can also recalc mid-prices, and the Risk module can double-check that current positions and orders are within limits before the next cycle.

Regular maintenance tasks: Every few minutes, the bot might perform housekeeping – e.g., computing and logging a PnL snapshot, checking memory usage, etc.. For example, every 5 minutes the bot could log a summary: “Open orders: X, Positions: Y, PnL: Z” and run a quick self-test (like ensure WebSocket is alive, described below).

Hourly/daily tasks: Perhaps hourly, the bot refreshes certain data: it could pull the latest market metadata (to catch if a market is resolved or new markets added) and verify the ban-status endpoint for compliance (Polymarket provides a GET /ban-status that indicates if the account is now restricted). If cert_required:true is ever seen, the bot would alert and likely stop trading that account (since a 14-day countdown to restriction has started) until resolved.

Throughout the loop, the system respects the risk management checks: before sending any order, it calls RiskLimits.validateOrder(order) – if that returns false or raises, the order is skipped and an alert or log is generated. This prevents unforeseen circumstances from causing an over-leveraged position.

WebSocket Reconnect & Data Integrity

The WebSocket connection is the lifeblood of the bot; thus, robust reconnect logic is crucial:

The bot implements an on_close handler for the WebSocket. If the connection drops or no message/ping is received in a certain timeframe, it will treat it as a disconnect.

Upon disconnect, the strategy should be paused (to avoid trading on stale data). The bot will not send new orders until the feed is back.

The bot then attempts to reconnect with an exponential backoff: e.g., wait 1s, then 2s, 4s, doubling each time up to perhaps 60s max, with some random jitter to avoid thundering herd if many bots reconnect. If reconnecting fails after, say, 10 attempts (~10 minutes), the bot will escalate the issue: send a critical alert (to Telegram/Discord) indicating it’s unable to stream data, and possibly restart itself or shut down gracefully.

On a successful reconnect, the bot will re-subscribe to the necessary channels (market and user). Immediately after reconnect, it should perform a full sync: call the REST order book for each market to get the latest state (in case any book changes were missed during downtime). It will then diff that with its last known state or just replace the local order book with the fresh snapshot.

Only after this resync does the bot resume normal operation, and it might initially place fresh quotes if the market moved in the interim.

This approach ensures that even if the WebSocket drops out (which can happen due to server issues or network blips), the bot doesn’t blindly continue on outdated info, and also doesn’t sit idle forever doing nothing – it will recover and continue trading as soon as possible, or alert humans if it cannot.

Order State Reconciliation

Polymarket being eventually consistent (and our bot possibly crashing or being restarted) means the bot must reconcile its view of orders with reality:

On startup (as noted), the bot fetches all open orders from the API. It cross-references this with any orders it had recorded in its local database that were marked live. If an order is in our DB as live but not returned by API, that means it was either filled or canceled while we were offline. The bot can further check the trade history endpoint to see if it was traded. It then updates the DB status of that order accordingly (e.g., mark as filled or canceled).

Conversely, if the API returns an open order that the bot didn’t have in its local memory (perhaps placed via another interface or due to a previous session’s unsaved state), the bot will add it to tracking (treat it as a live order it should manage or at least know about).

During runtime, a periodic reconciliation (maybe every 10 minutes) can run: call GET /open-orders and ensure each matches what the bot thinks is open. If an order that bot thought open is missing, likely it got canceled externally (or partially filled and removed). The bot will then adjust accordingly (stop waiting on that order, adjust position if partially filled, etc.).

This redundancy (both listening to WebSocket and periodically polling) ensures no order fills or cancellations are missed. It guards against edge cases like the WebSocket skipping a message or the bot process being down for a short time – when it comes back up, it heals the state.

Circuit Breakers and Emergency Halts

Manual Kill-Switch: We implement an admin-accessible kill-switch. For instance, if the bot is running on a server, an HTTP endpoint like /admin/kill or even a specific file/DB field can trigger a shutdown. When activated (could be via a curl command or a message), the bot will cancel all open orders immediately, cease placing new ones, close out positions if feasible, and then stop the strategy loop. This allows an operator (or an automated watchdog) to quickly stop trading in an emergency (e.g., discovered bug or market crash).

Automated Circuit Breakers: As part of risk management, the bot continuously monitors certain conditions to decide if it should auto-halt or temporarily pause trading:

PnL Drawdown: If the bot’s cumulative loss for the day exceeds a threshold (say 5% of capital or a fixed $ amount), it triggers a halt. This prevents a runaway scenario where the bot keeps losing money in a vicious cycle. The bot will cancel orders and not resume for a cooldown period (maybe 1 hour), after which it could re-enable trading (optionally requiring manual review).

High Error Rates: If the Polymarket API starts erroring a lot (e.g., many 500 errors or timeouts, or consecutive 429 rate limits beyond acceptable), this could indicate an API issue. The bot might pause trading for a few minutes if >20% of requests in a short window are failing. Trading in the dark against a flaky API can be dangerous (orders might be not actually placed, etc.).

WebSocket Instability: Similarly, if the WebSocket disconnects too frequently (say 5+ times in 10 minutes), something is wrong with the network or feed. The bot can halt new trading for a bit, only resuming when the connection stabilizes.

Market Volatility Spike: Polymarket markets can sometimes have erratic moves (especially low-liquidity ones). If the bot detects, for instance, a >30% price swing within a minute, it might be wise to cancel orders on that market and wait. Such swings might be due to news or manipulation – either way, pausing prevents the bot from providing liquidity at stale prices. The bot can resume for that market after things calm down.

All such events are logged and trigger alerts. The resume logic can be automatic after a set cooldown (e.g., resume after 10 minutes for volatility, or next day for PnL drawdown reset), possibly with some conditions (like only resume if manual intervention marks it safe, depending on how conservative we want to be).

Idempotency and Duplication Safeguards

Network issues or crashes can sometimes cause duplicate actions. The bot employs idempotent patterns to avoid, for example, placing the same order twice or double-counting a fill:

Order UID Tracking: Each order the bot intends to place can be tagged with a unique client-side ID (UUID). The bot logs this in a local database before sending the order. If a network error happens during submission, the bot can query open orders to see if that order might have actually gone through (perhaps the API succeeded but the response didn’t reach us). By matching on price/size or a client ID if supported, it avoids accidentally sending it again. The Polymarket API might not have a client-provided ID field, so this is primarily handled by careful reconciliation after any uncertain outcome.

Trade Deduplication: When recording trades from WebSocket, use the trade’s unique ID or the order ID + fill info as a composite key in the database. This way, if the same fill message is received twice (maybe due to a reconnect replay), the bot will recognize it already processed that trade and ignore the duplicate.

Position Updates: When updating positions in a database, use atomic operations or compare-and-swap logic (for SQL: WHERE clauses to ensure the row hasn’t changed unexpectedly). This prevents race conditions where two events simultaneously try to adjust position and one overwrites the other. While single-threaded node should handle sequentially, it’s good to be safe if using multiple workers or threads in future.

These practices ensure consistency – the bot’s internal accounting should remain correct even under retry scenarios.

Monitoring & Alerting

We tie in monitoring to every critical part of the system:

Metrics collection: As discussed, the bot exposes runtime metrics. This includes standard ones like memory usage, event loop lag, etc., and custom ones like bot_pnl_total, orders_active_count, websocket_reconnect_count, etc. These can be visualized on dashboards and used for alert conditions.

External uptime monitoring: The bot can integrate with a service like healthchecks.io or a simple cron job that expects a heartbeat. For example, the bot could ping an external URL every minute to signal liveness. If the heartbeat stops (meaning the bot likely hung or crashed), the external service notifies the operators.

Alert channels: We use multiple channels for different severity levels:

Critical (e.g., trading halted, major crash): Immediate Telegram message to the developer or ops team. This ensures we see it on mobile quickly.

Warnings: Perhaps send to a Discord channel or email for less urgent issues, like temporary disconnects that auto-recovered, or “no trades in last 24h on market X” (indicating maybe something is off but not urgent).

Info/Reports: Daily PnL summary emailed or posted to a Discord channel for record-keeping and analysis.

Specific triggers: Alerts include: Circuit breaker tripped (with reason), Unexpected error/exception caught, Reconnect failed X times, Low wallet balance (e.g., if gas or USDC is low), or compliance flag (cert_required seen or region blocked). The compliance one is very important – if the bot ever sees a response indicating it’s not allowed to trade (perhaps Polymarket updated their restrictions), it should immediately stop and alert.

Watchdog & Crash handling: We likely run the bot inside a process manager (systemd or PM2). If it crashes, it will be automatically restarted. Upon restart, it should alert that it experienced an outage (so we know to investigate cause). If it crashes repeatedly (say more than 3 times in a short span), the process manager might stop trying – but our external heartbeat failing would catch that and escalate.

Backup and Recovery Procedures

For long-term operation, we plan for data durability and quick recovery:

Database backups: If using SQLite, setup a daily job to copy the DB file to secure cloud storage. If using Postgres, do regular dumps or use managed backups. Given the small size (trades/PnL logs), these backups are cheap and can be automated (e.g., upload to an S3 bucket every night). Retain backups for at least 30 days in case of needed audit or to restore from a point-in-time.

Server failure recovery: Document the steps to redeploy the bot on a new server if the current one fails. Essentially: provision a new VM, install Docker or Node environment, pull the code from version control, restore the latest DB backup, put the config/secrets in place, and start the bot. The process should take less than an hour if well-prepared. Using infrastructure-as-code or scripts for setup can greatly speed this up.

Key management: Ensure the wallet’s private key and API secrets are backed up securely (in an encrypted form) so that if we need to move to new hardware, we can get the bot running with the same identity. Also possibly maintain a hot spare wallet with some funds in case the primary wallet gets compromised or blacklisted – though if we follow all precautions, that shouldn’t happen.

Scaling & Redundancy: At small scale, a single instance is fine. If someday running with more capital, we might consider a secondary failover bot that can take over if primary fails (with appropriate coordination to not double-place orders). But for now, a single-instance with auto-restart and good alerts suffices.

This runbook, in summary, ensures that operating the bot is not a black box. We have clear steps for startup, know how to verify it’s healthy, and have plans for handling anything from a minor hiccup (temporary disconnect) to major events (market outages, or hitting limits) and even external changes (regulatory blocks). The aim is for the bot to be as autonomous and self-correcting as possible, but with enough transparency and hooks that human operators are always aware of its status and can intervene when needed.

Compliance Checklist

Operating a Polymarket bot involves significant compliance and safety considerations. Below is a checklist of items to ensure we abide by all rules and mitigate regulatory or ethical risks:

Geographic Restrictions:

 Permitted Region: Verify that the bot’s server and controlling user are in a country not on Polymarket’s blocked list (33 fully banned countries including the US, UK, France, etc.). Also check that the country is not “close-only” (like Singapore, Poland, Thailand, Taiwan) which would restrict opening new positions. If in close-only region, do not run the bot (or only allow it to close existing positions).

 Ban-Status API Check: On startup and at least daily, call GET /ban-status with the bot’s address. If the response indicates cert_required: true or any restriction flag, immediately halt trading and send an alert. cert_required means Polymarket requires additional KYC/verification within ~14 days for that user. In such a case, address it (complete verification) or shut down before the deadline to avoid forced closure.

 No Bypassing Geo-block: Do not attempt to use VPNs or proxies to circumvent Polymarket’s geofence. This is against the Terms of Service. The bot’s IP should consistently resolve to the allowed region. If relocating servers, re-check regional permissions.

Security of Keys & Credentials:

 Private Key Protection: Never store the wallet’s private key in plain text on the server. It should come from an encrypted file or environment variable. The repository will have .env.example but the actual .env with secrets is never committed.

 Access Control: Limit access to the server. Use SSH key authentication (disable password login). If using a cloud VM, ensure security groups/firewalls only allow necessary traffic (e.g., no open database ports).

 Key Rotation: Plan to rotate API credentials or even the private key every so often (perhaps every 6-12 months) in case of any potential exposure. This may involve moving funds to a new address and updating Polymarket API keys.

 Secure Dependencies: Keep the bot’s libraries (especially those handling cryptography or wallets like ethers.js) up to date to avoid any known vulnerabilities.

Financial Risk and Disclosure:

 Capital Allocation: Only fund the bot with an amount you are willing to lose (e.g., $50 as initial live test). Clearly label this in documentation. No external or user funds are involved (this bot is proprietary trading of personal funds).

 Disclosures: If the code is ever shared or open-sourced, include disclaimers: “For educational purposes, no guarantee of profit, use at your own risk”. Trading is risky; even though we attempt to mitigate risk, losses are possible. We log all trades for transparency and potential tax reporting.

 Start in Simulation: As planned, run the bot in paper trading for an extended period (>1 week) before risking real money. Only transition to small real trades after proving the strategy in simulation.

 Limit Losses: Set conservative loss limits (via circuit breakers, etc.) so the maximum daily loss is contained (for instance, if we say max daily loss $10 on a $100 capital, we enforce that).

Rate Limiting and Politeness:

 API Throttle: Respect Polymarket’s API usage guidelines. Implement backoff on HTTP 429 responses and do not spam the endpoints. Use the WebSocket for frequent data to reduce REST calls.

 Batching: Where possible, batch operations (Polymarket allows posting multiple orders in one request) to reduce number of API calls. The code should never attempt more than the allowed threshold (we assume ~60 orders/min) – our strategy naturally stays far below this with small capital.

 Health: Monitor Polymarket’s status (if there’s an outage or maintenance, the bot should ideally pause rather than hammer retries).

Testing & Quality Assurance:

 Unit Tests: Develop unit tests for critical logic (orderbook parsing, price calculations, risk check functions). These tests should be run before any deployment or after any code change to catch bugs early.

 Integration Tests: If possible, test the bot on a Polymarket staging environment or in a dry-run mode that hits real APIs but perhaps with small orders that immediately cancel. This can reveal any issues with auth or order submission in a safe way.

 Backtesting: Use recorded market data to backtest the strategy over historical scenarios. This helps refine parameters and uncover how the bot would have behaved during, say, a big market move or a prolonged stagnant period.

 Chaos Testing: Simulate failure scenarios (disconnects, high latency, DB errors) in a controlled environment to ensure the bot’s recovery logic works (this was partly done in development, but it’s a continuous process).

 Pre-Launch Checklist: Go through a final checklist (could be similar to this one) before turning the bot live: Are balances sufficient? Is ban-status clear? Did we run the bot in paper mode with no issues for several days? Are monitoring alerts set up?.

Monitoring & Maintenance:

 Live Monitoring: Keep Grafana or another dashboard open when the bot is initially launched with real money. Watch the metrics – if anything looks abnormal (CPU usage spikes, memory leak, PnL plunging), be ready to intervene.

 Alerts Verified: Test that a critical alert (e.g., manually trigger a circuit breaker or kill-switch) indeed sends a notification to your device. You don’t want to discover alert misconfiguration only after something bad happened unnoticed.

 Incident Response Plan: Document what to do in common incidents: e.g., If bot crashes – check logs, possibly restart; If large loss incurred – probably stop trading and analyze before restart; If API key compromised – revoke and generate a new one, etc. Having these written down means less scrambling under stress.

 Logging: Ensure log files are rotated and archived (to prevent disk full issues). Also periodically review logs to catch any small errors or warnings that might not have triggered alerts but could indicate a potential problem.

Legal and Ethical Compliance:

 Legality: Confirm that participating in prediction markets is legal in your jurisdiction. Polymarket’s geoblock is one layer, but you are responsible for ensuring local laws (some places treat it as gambling or require it to be licensed). If uncertain, seek legal advice.

 Tax Reporting: Keep records of all trades (the bot’s database and logs serve this purpose). Profits may be subject to tax, so we maintain an audit trail. Using the DB export, one can compute net gains for reporting. This also means not trading in ways that obfuscate transactions – transparency is maintained.

 No Market Manipulation: Program the bot to follow ethical trading practices. For instance, no spoofing (placing fake orders with no intent to trade, just to mislead others), no wash trading (trading with oneself to generate volume). The bot’s strategies should aim to provide genuine liquidity or arbitrage fairly available opportunities.

 No Frontrunning or Exploiting Non-Public Info: If the bot operator has privileged information (unlikely in a public market, but for completeness), that could be illegal insider trading in some contexts. The bot will only use public data streams.

 Community Compliance: Adhere to Polymarket’s Terms of Service and developer guidelines. If Polymarket requests cessation of certain activities (perhaps if a bot is deemed harmful to market integrity), comply promptly.

This checklist should be reviewed regularly, especially if Polymarket updates their policies or if the bot’s scope changes (e.g. increasing capital or adding new data sources). By following these points, we aim to run the trading bot responsibly, safely, and within all legal boundaries.

Deployment & Monitoring Infrastructure

Deploying the Polymarket bot for continuous operation requires careful choice of infrastructure to balance cost, reliability, and performance. Below we outline the deployment plan and monitoring setup:

Hosting Environment: We will run the bot on a cloud virtual machine (VM) with a Linux OS (e.g., Ubuntu 22.04). Given the small capital and moderate workload, even a modest VM is sufficient. Based on cost analysis, a 2 vCPU, 2-4 GB RAM machine is recommended for stable operations. For example, a DigitalOcean Droplet ($5–$6/month for 1vCPU/1GB or $15 for 2vCPU/2GB) or Hetzner Cloud CX21 (around $5 for 2vCPU/4GB) would be cost-effective. To be safe, we lean toward a mid-range option (~$15/month) which provides headroom for running Docker, databases, and monitoring tools together. The server should be in a region close to Polymarket’s servers (which are in eu-west-2 London for primary) – so an EU datacenter (e.g., Frankfurt or London) is ideal to minimize latency.

Docker & Containerization: We will containerize the application for consistency across environments. A Dockerfile will be written to encapsulate the Node.js app, including the Polymarket SDK and all dependencies. We’ll also use a docker-compose setup in development to run auxiliary services like Prometheus and Grafana, and this can be mirrored in production. The container ensures that if we migrate hosts or need to scale, the environment differences won’t cause issues. The Docker image will be built on a lightweight Node base (e.g., node:18-alpine) to keep it small. We'll mount volumes or use persistent storage for the SQLite database and logs, so they survive container restarts.

Process Management: For running the bot container continuously, we have options: run Docker directly (with restart policy always), or use a process manager like systemd on the host for the Docker compose stack. Alternatively, one could use Kubernetes or Docker Swarm, but that’s overkill for a single bot. Simplicity is key, so likely we’ll use Docker Compose with a restart policy and maybe an overseer script. If not containerizing, using PM2 or systemd to run the Node process is fine too. It should be set to start on boot, so if the VM restarts, the bot comes up automatically.

Monitoring Stack: We plan to use Prometheus and Grafana for monitoring. Two approaches:

Self-hosted: Run Prometheus and Grafana in Docker containers on the same VM (since the load is small). Grafana’s memory use might be a bit high for a tiny VM, so that pushes us toward at least 2GB RAM on the VM. We can restrict retention and scrape frequency to keep Prometheus slim. Grafana will provide a web UI for us to see charts (we’ll secure it with a password).

Cloud-based: Alternatively, use Grafana Cloud free tier for metrics, as it offers 10k series and 50GB logs for $0. In that case, we’d run a lightweight Grafana agent on the VM to push metrics to Grafana Cloud. This might actually save resources versus running full Prometheus locally. Given cost is zero, and setup is not too complex, Grafana Cloud is appealing. We just have to ensure no sensitive data is in metrics (they’ll be mostly numeric, so fine).

We will also set up alerting integration in Grafana (or use Alertmanager if self-hosted Prometheus). Alerts like those defined in the runbook (e.g., bot down, PnL drawdown) can be configured as PromQL rules and tied to contact points (Telegram via a bot API, email, etc.).

Logging: The bot’s logs will be written to file and also could be sent to a cloud log service. For example, Grafana Cloud can also ingest logs (50GB free is plenty for text logs). If we send logs there, we can easily search them in case of incidents. If not, we at least ensure logrotate is configured on the VM to archive and compress old logs. Key logs might also be tailed by a tool like lnav or forwarded to Slack/Discord for significant events (the alerting system covers the main ones; logs are more for debugging).

Backups: We will implement an automated backup for the SQLite database. A daily cron job (or a small script run via systemd timer) will dump the DB and upload it to a secure storage (could be AWS S3, or even email it encrypted somewhere, given it’s small). Alternatively, since the DB is small, using a free Postgres (like Supabase free tier) could avoid needing backups (the cloud provider would handle it). The plan is to start with SQLite for MVP/V1 and then consider moving to a managed Postgres as the project matures (cost ~$15/mo for a tiny managed DB).

Deployment Workflow: For code updates, we’ll use Git. Possibly set up a CI/CD pipeline: when code is pushed to main branch on GitHub, have GitHub Actions build the Docker image and maybe push to a private registry. Then the server could pull the new image and restart the container with minimal downtime. Given this is a personal bot, a simpler approach is fine: manually git pull on the server and restart the process. The key is to not lose track of open orders during deployment. To handle that, our restart sequence (with state reconciliation) will ensure no duplicate orders. We might do deployments when the bot has no open orders (or we trigger the kill-switch first, wait for all orders to cancel, then update code, then start). This ensures a safe handover.

Networking and Security: The VM will only expose necessary ports. Likely:

The Prometheus metrics port (if we want to scrape it externally) or Grafana agent port.

Possibly an SSH port for our access.

If Grafana is local and we want to see the dashboard, either tunnel it or open Grafana’s port with proper firewall rules.

If we run a simple HTTP endpoint for kill-switch or health, secure it (either bind to localhost so we call via SSH tunnel, or require an API token in the request).

We’ll also enable basic firewalls (ufw or cloud firewall) to restrict access.

Uptime Monitoring: As mentioned, a heartbeat to an external service can complement our stack. For instance, use healthchecks.io by adding a curl in the bot’s code every minute or as a cron. If the bot stops heartbeating, we get notified. This is a sanity check in case the entire VM goes down (where our internal Prometheus wouldn’t be able to alert if the machine is off).

Resource Considerations: The bot itself is not extremely CPU heavy – a single Node thread handling a few markets, mostly I/O bound. 1 vCPU is usually fine. But the WebSocket handling and rapid messages might occasionally spike CPU. 2 vCPUs gives breathing room especially when combined with Prom/Grafana on the same box. Memory: Node might use <100MB; add Prom (~100-200MB) and Grafana (~200+ MB), so 1GB might be tight, 2GB is safer. Disk: minimal (just logs and DB, a few MBs). We’ll ensure swap is enabled (so the system doesn’t OOM if usage bursts).

Costs: Summarizing expected monthly costs for this setup:

VM: ~$15 (mid-tier cloud VM).

Monitoring: $0 (Grafana Cloud free or self-hosted).

Database: $0 (SQLite or free Postgres tier).

Gas fees for Polygon transactions: maybe $2–$10 depending on activity (these are on-chain costs for settlements, approvals).

Total: On the order of $20-$30/month, which is reasonable for a hobby project, but it means if using only $100 capital, one needs to earn >20% annual just to break even on costs. This is a known challenge for micro-scale bots. We mitigate by keeping infrastructure lean.

Deployment Timeline: We will likely deploy the MVP in a paper trading mode on the server in Week 2 to test the environment and monitoring. Once stable, we’ll deploy the V1 live version by end of Week 4. From then on, it’s about monitoring and incremental improvements.

Scaling Up: In the future, if we increase capital or markets, we might upgrade the VM or separate concerns (e.g., database on a separate service, etc.). If latency becomes crucial (for event trading), we might move the server to even closer location or a provider with better ping. For now, our design should comfortably run on a single modest server.

In summary, the deployment uses a single-node architecture with all components co-located for simplicity: the trading bot, a time-series DB (Prometheus), and a dashboard (Grafana). This is cost-efficient and adequate for a small project. With robust monitoring and alerts set up, we ensure that we can respond quickly to any issues in the production environment, thereby keeping the bot running smoothly and safely.

